/-
# Non-linear locally finite simple groups with bounded p-subgroups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 4.37)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 4.37 (O. H. Kegel): is there an infinite
locally finite simple group `G` that cannot be represented by matrices over a field and is such that
for some prime `p` the `p`-subgroups of `G` are either of bounded derived length or of finite
exponent?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.PGroup
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Solvable
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup.Defs
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.GroupClassesShared

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.NonlinearLocallyFiniteSimpleKourovka4Problem37Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 4.37 (O. H.
Kegel). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700245 (`KOU-4.37`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Representable by matrices means a faithful homomorphism into `GL_n(K)` for some field `K` and some
`n`. Both alternatives are read uniformly over the `p`-subgroups: a common bound `d` on the derived
length (`derivedSeries P d = ⊥`), or a common exponent `p^k` (the reading of "finite exponent" that
makes the refuted claim weakest). Locally finite (the subgroup generated by every finite set is
finite) is `IsLocallyFinite` of `GroupClassesShared`.
-/

open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.GroupClassesShared (IsLocallyFinite)

/-- `G` can be represented by matrices over a field: it embeds in `GL_n(K)` for some field `K` and
some `n`. -/
def IsLinear (G : Type*) [Group G] : Prop :=
  ∃ (K : Type) (_ : Field K) (n : ℕ) (f : G →* Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup (Fin n) K),
    Function.Injective f

/-- Kourovka 4.37 (O. H. Kegel): there is an infinite locally finite simple group `G` that cannot
be represented by matrices over a field and such that, for some prime `p`, either the
`p`-subgroups of `G` have bounded derived length or they have bounded exponent (all dividing a
fixed `p^k`). -/
def existsNonlinearLocallyFiniteSimpleBoundedPSubgroups : Prop :=
  ∃ (G : Type) (_ : Group G), Infinite G ∧ IsSimpleGroup G ∧ IsLocallyFinite G ∧ ¬ IsLinear G ∧
    ∃ p : ℕ, p.Prime ∧
      ((∃ d : ℕ, ∀ P : Subgroup G, IsPGroup p P → derivedSeries P d = ⊥) ∨
        ∃ k : ℕ, ∀ P : Subgroup G, IsPGroup p P → ∀ x ∈ P, x ^ (p ^ k) = 1)

/--
Resolved false, modulo the classification of finite simple groups: the Kourovka Archive answers 4.37
"No (mod CFSG) by Theorem 4.8 of Kegel and Wehrfritz": no infinite locally finite simple group that
cannot be represented by matrices over a field has, for some prime `p`, `p`-subgroups of bounded
derived length or of bounded exponent. Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved
Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of
Solved Problems, Problem 4.37 (O. H. Kegel); resolution: O. H. Kegel and B. A. F. Wehrfritz, Locally
finite groups, North-Holland Mathematical Library 3, North-Holland, Amsterdam, 1973, Theorem 4.8,
combined with the classification of finite simple groups; Kourovka Archive of Solved Problems,
Problem 4.37: No (mod CFSG) by Theorem 4.8 of Kegel and Wehrfritz,
https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v49.
-/
public theorem_wanted existsNonlinearLocallyFiniteSimpleBoundedPSubgroups_refuted :
    ¬ existsNonlinearLocallyFiniteSimpleBoundedPSubgroups

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.NonlinearLocallyFiniteSimpleKourovka4Problem37Wanted
